# Member summary model candidate

## Problem

The member screen currently gives its overview a full column, repeats currency sections, and displays declaration and funding counts. `EntityDetails.svelte` loads parsed `MemberDetails`, renders identity, and chooses summary or history. `MemberSummary.svelte` renders API summary totals and categories. History and funder screens share `entity-details.ts`, so changing its transport contracts or `formatAmount` would expand this work. The new summary needs one GBP total in the identity header, a category graph, and a weekly earnings graph. The API supplies registration dates, so the weekly graph must say "Earnings registered per week" and "Week registered" rather than claim when payments occurred.

## Usage

`EntityDetails.svelte` derives one view model after a member response. It passes the total to the identity and the graphs to the summary. Svelte components only render their inputs.

```ts
import { memberSummary, type MemberSummaryModel } from "./lib/member-summary";

// Within the loaded member branch, or a derived value scoped to it.
const summary = memberSummary(details);
// <MemberIdentity member={details.member} view={view} total={summary.total} />
// <MemberSummary memberId={details.member.id} summary={summary} />
```

The header total stays visible on the history route because the user asked for the top right of the member page. The summary graph components receive no raw entries. Existing recent declarations can remain below the graphs, if retained, with the action "View declaration history" and no declaration count. The totals and graphs do not need prose context blocks.

## Shape

Add `frontend/src/lib/member-summary.ts`. It owns GBP selection, exact decimal arithmetic, category presentation, earnings category membership, registration week bucketing, missing amount states, and graph scaling. Keep its decimal representation and helper functions private. Export one builder and its returned model types.

```ts
export type SummaryValue =
  | { kind: "known"; text: string }
  | { kind: "partial"; text: string }
  | { kind: "unavailable"; text: "Amount unavailable" }
  | { kind: "empty"; text: "No GBP amounts" };

export interface CategoryPoint {
  id: number;
  label: string;
  value: SummaryValue;
  // Coordinates in a shared signed scale, always finite and bounded.
  from: number;
  to: number;
}
export interface RegisteredWeek {
  start: string; // Monday YYYY-MM-DD in UTC
  label: string;
  value: SummaryValue;
  from: number;
  to: number;
}
export interface MemberSummaryModel {
  total: SummaryValue;
  categories: readonly CategoryPoint[];
  categoryZero: number;
  earnings: {
    kind: "ready" | "empty" | "unavailable";
    weeks: readonly RegisteredWeek[];
    zero: number;
    hasUndatedAmounts: boolean;
  };
}
export function memberSummary(details: MemberDetails): MemberSummaryModel {
  // not implemented
}
```

`SummaryValue` preserves the distinction between zero, partial knowledge, unavailable amounts, and no GBP data. It does not export counts for the UI. A partial value can expose a short accessible "Known amount" label without restoring the removed coverage blocks. For unknown-only weeks, the SVG uses an unavailable marker rather than a zero bar. A missing week between dated earnings declarations represents zero registered earnings, not an amount inferred for an undated declaration.

The builder uses the server's GBP summary amount and categories for the total/category graph. This maintains existing authoritative aggregate behavior. It builds weekly earnings from declarations whose category IDs are 1, 2, or 12 and entries whose currency equals GBP. That finite earnings rule exists in one private predicate. It never matches human category names. The API remains the source of category names and IDs.

Private decimal arithmetic uses a signed `bigint` coefficient with a decimal scale. Addition aligns scales before summing, retains all decimal digits, and canonicalizes signed zero. Formatting adds £ without converting amounts to `number`. Only bounded normalized graph coordinates become numbers. Currency presentation is owned here, so history and funder pages retain their current format.

Registration timestamps are already validated by the transport parser. Convert each date to a UTC Monday at midnight. Weeks run continuously from the first dated qualifying GBP earnings declaration to the last, with zero weeks in gaps. Null dates never receive an invented week. Known undated values stay in the header and category totals but cannot enter the weekly series. Null amounts contribute no money and mark their bucket unavailable or partial. Negative entries remain negative. Known zero dates produce a real zero point and establish the range. There is no use of the global summary registration range because gifts could stretch an earnings graph beyond its earnings dates.

This is a deep module with one operation. It hides exact arithmetic and time rules from rendering while returning the values required for header and graphs. It avoids a mutable store, transport changes, and a sequence of helpers that components must coordinate.

## Layout and file scope

`MemberIdentity.svelte` accepts the derived total. Use a header grid with member title and metadata on the left, an end-aligned total on the right, and tabs spanning the width. The total label is "Declared value" and the value is £2,323,720.45 for member 5091. On narrow widths it moves below identity content.

`MemberSummary.svelte` replaces its overview column and repeated currencies with two sections. The category section uses a horizontal graph because its category labels are long. The weekly section uses a compact SVG with a visible zero baseline, signed bars, readable sparse date ticks, and per-week accessible labels. A table or disclosure listing exact weekly amounts gives keyboard users access to every point. The two graphs can occupy equal desktop columns, with categories allowed enough width for wrapped labels. Stack them below 800px. Existing CSS already defines the 800px summary breakpoint and signed category bars; revise those member selectors and remove orphaned overview selectors. Funder selectors stay unchanged.

Files changed: the new model module, `EntityDetails.svelte`, `MemberIdentity.svelte`, `MemberSummary.svelte`, member rules in `styles.css`, and `tests/entity-details.spec.ts`. Add model tests only if the project's existing test runner can directly exercise TS modules without a new testing dependency; otherwise cover boundary cases through mocked responses in the existing Playwright suite.

## Alternatives considered

Component local calculations would place currency policy in the header and weekly policy in the summary, with chart math split across components. The proposed builder gives both components the same derived model through one call.

A changed API response with weekly aggregates would centralize earnings policy server side, but requires Rust domain and serialization changes and expands scope beyond this frontend request. It also changes the response for consumers that still need all currencies.

A Svelte store for member aggregates would introduce lifetime and reactive ownership despite the loaded detail response already being immutable for the page. A pure builder needs no subscriptions or synchronization.

## Tradeoffs accepted

- We accept the existing API summary as the source for total/category amounts and raw declarations as the source for weeks. The real fixture proves those representations have different coverage, so tests must not require their grand totals to match.
- We accept category IDs 1, 2, and 12 as a single documented frontend earnings classifier until the API exposes that domain meaning.
- We accept explicit missing states in the model without user-facing count blocks. A value of zero must not disguise unknown data.

## Verification evidence and plan

Read `MemberSummary.svelte`, `MemberIdentity.svelte`, `EntityDetails.svelte`, `entity-details.ts`, member CSS, and relevant Playwright assertions. Current tests at `entity-details.spec.ts:733` explicitly expect three currency totals, a signed negative category, and a currency coverage note; replace these summary assertions while preserving history and funder behavior tests. Existing amounts exceed JavaScript safe integer precision and have nine decimal places, so that fixture remains valuable for exact formatting.

Read `/tmp/exposed-member-5091.json` with Python Decimal. Its API summary gives GBP 2323720.45. Categories 1 and 2 sum to 2057097.84; declaration categories include 12 even though its GBP summary categories do not. Weekly classification must include category 12 without assuming every such declaration has a known amount. These observations ground the design; no implementation was executed.

Verify exact sums with 0.1 plus 0.2, large amounts and arbitrary scales; dated negative and zero values; Sunday/Monday and timezone offset transitions; continuous gap weeks; partial and unknown-only buckets; all-undated earnings; empty and non-GBP-only summaries; category 12; and gift exclusion from weeks. Run existing frontend check, build, and relevant Playwright tests. Drive real member 5091 and capture desktop screenshots from the implementation worktree. If publishing a PR, upload those as GitHub attachments and inspect the rendered description as required by AGENTS.md.

## Synthesis decision

Candidate only. The parent architect chooses the base and records grafts after comparing candidates.

## Implementation reconciliation

No implementation performed. All repository inspection was read only.

## Next implementation step

Implement the pure member summary builder and its edge-case verification before changing Svelte markup.
EOF","max_output_tokens":1000}));

